import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How Oli's Bookshelf builds reading-order lists: publication first, chronology second, sources on the page."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How the lists are made</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A reading-order page is a research note. Publication order is the default first read.
      Chronological exceptions get explained without dumping spoilers into the table.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>The working order</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Start with the author’s or publisher’s list</strong> when one exists. Martha Wells’
        Murderbot page and the official Discworld novel list are the model.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Separate novels from optional shorts.</strong> A picture book, an RPG extra, or a
        web-only short does not belong in the numbered novel column.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Publication first for a first read.</strong> Chronological prequels often exist
        because they lean on later knowledge. We flag those instead of silently reordering.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Name the common mistakes.</strong> “I read Fugitive Telemetry after Network Effect
        because it was published later” is a useful sentence. “Just read them in any order” is not.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Write next-reads from the reason, not the algorithm.</strong> Three to five titles,
        each with an original sentence about voice, structure, or aftercare. No scraped “readers
        also liked.”
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Leave uncertainties on the page.</strong> Dates, collected editions, and retailer
        blurbs go stale. If an order is contested, the guide says so instead of inventing a piety.
        <strong>Date the sources.</strong> Collected editions, retailer blurbs, and official URLs
        move. Each guide records the day we last opened its sources.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What counts as a source</h2>
    <p>
      Author sites, publisher reading-order pages, and bibliographic publication records. Fandom
      wikis can point at a primary source; they are not the source. We put the URL on the guide.
    </p>

    <h2>What we will not invent</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Plot summaries that retell a chapter</li>
      <li>Star ratings or “best book in the series”</li>
      <li>Income or traffic claims</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
